    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I'm sorry to hear the garrison metaphor didn't quite fit, but I can certainly provide the specific clinical details you need for the incision care and monitoring signs. Please note that while I can offer general veterinary guidance, this information should not replace the advice of your local veterinarian who knows your specific herd and the animal's condition.\n\n**Incision Site Sanitation Protocol**\nTo minimize the risk of secondary infection, the incision site requires a strict hygiene routine, similar to how a garrison might disinfect a breach point before reinforcing it.\n*   **Preparation:** Gather sterile saline solution, povidone-iodine (Betadine) or chlorhexidine, sterile gauze, and clean gloves.\n*   **Procedure:** Gently clean the incision line and surrounding skin using sterile saline to remove any debris or exudate. Avoid scrubbing the wound itself; instead, wipe from the center of the incision outward in a circular motion. Apply a thin layer of antiseptic solution (if prescribed by your vet) and cover with a sterile, non-adherent dressing.\n*   **Frequency:** Change the dressing daily or more frequently if the site becomes wet or soiled.\n\n**Sign",
      "source": "target",
